Track 2: Khel-Jinn's dry canteen
- Story & Context: Khel-Jinn sat by the fire, obsessively shaking his canteen, convinced the rhythm of the water inside was a coded map to a water-well that hasn't existed for a century. He claims he met a man in the ruins who traded him a synth lead for a single drop of clean water. He cries into the mic during the chorus, his voice raspy from years of inhaling fine dust. "The thirst sounds better than the rain."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A cracked, clay canteen resting on hot, shifting dunes with heat haze distorting the landscape, cinematic desaturated colors, gritty textures.

Track 3: Molten furnace glass drips downward
- Story & Context: After the fire that leveled the central market of the caravan, the survivors gathered the cooling globs of fused silicon and melted synthesizers, calling it the "Great Hardening." They placed the chunks on their instruments to act as filters for the hum, believing the sound of the cooling glass acts as a lullaby for the spirits of the dead machines. It is said that if you listen closely, you can hear the machines still trying to process their final, fatal sequence. "Nothing is lost, only melted into time."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A molten, semi-transparent blob of glass cooling on a jagged metallic surface, internal light pulsing with deep violet and gold hues, abstract, organic, surreal.

